float strategyMinRiskSurfaceScale = input.float(0.00, "Minimum risk-surface scale to admit a trade", minval = 0.00, maxval = 1.00, step = 0.05, group = G_RISK,
     tooltip = "Entry admission gate, separate from the floor above. DEFAULTS TO OFF (0.00). Two independent live Strategy Tester re-tests (thresholds 0.30 and 0.15, same BTCUSDT.P 15m window) both underperformed the ungated baseline: 93 trades / +73.40 USD / PF 3.64 versus ~46-49 trades / ~+52 USD / PF ~2.3 at either threshold. Root cause: X is bimodal (43 of 93 trades sit exactly on the 0.10 floor, only 6 sit in 0.10-0.30, 44 sit >=0.30), so 0.15 and 0.30 admit almost the identical trade set - this is not tunable in that range. Because pyramiding=0, skipping a trade leaves the position flat sooner and lets a later, otherwise-blocked signal fire instead; both re-tests independently admitted the same new trade (2026-06-01 11:00, H~69 near eveCounterRegimeMin, CATASTROPHE SL -15.98) that never existed in the ungated run. strategyLongMemoryScale/strategyShortMemoryScale (Adaptive Capital Intelligence) additionally resize every later trade on a side from the running realized win/loss memory, so even matched trades close with a different $ result. Net conclusion: gating entries on this score reduces realized profit on this window; keep at 0.00 unless a future re-test on a wider sample shows otherwise. The mechanism (skip strategy.entry when longRiskSurfaceScale/shortRiskSurfaceScale falls below this value, changing nothing else) is left in place for further research.")
